#: ../../../abi_policy.md:3
msgid "ABI and Public Header Policy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:5
msgid ""
"LLVM-libc defines its own ABI. This page explains how the layout of public "
"types is chosen, which symbols the library exports, and how public headers "
"are written. It applies to full builds, where LLVM-libc provides the "
"headers. In {ref}`overlay mode <overlay_mode>` the headers and the ABI "
"belong to the system libc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:11
msgid "See {ref}`design_philosophy` for why the project works this way."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:13
msgid "Nothing is frozen"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:15
msgid ""
"LLVM-libc currently makes no ABI stability promise. The layouts of public "
"types, the names of internal symbols, and the values of implementation-"
"defined macros may change between two commits. Because the library is {ref}"
"`linked statically into the program it was built for <design_philosophy>`, "
"such a change does not break existing binaries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:21
msgid ""
"This lack of stability gives the project the freedom to change the ABI "
"without fear of breaking users. If there's a mistake in the ABI, it can be "
"fixed quickly and uncontroversially."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:25
msgid ""
"The project intends to keep this freedom for as long as possible. A change "
"that would make part of the ABI hard to change later needs discussion with "
"the maintainers before it is written."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:31
msgid "Choosing the layout of a public type"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:33
msgid ""
"When a public type is added or changed, its layout is decided by the first "
"of these that applies:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:36
msgid ""
"**The kernel.** If the type is passed to or received from a system call "
"without conversion, it must have the layout the kernel expects. `struct "
"timespec` and `stack_t` are examples."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:39
msgid ""
"**LLVM-libc.** Otherwise, design the type for LLVM-libc's implementation, "
"and convert at the system call boundary where necessary. `struct stat` is "
"LLVM-libc's own layout and is filled in from `statx` (`src/__support/OSUtil/"
"linux/stat/`). `struct sigaction` is converted to and from the kernel's "
"layout (`KernelSigaction` in `src/signal/linux/signal_utils.h`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:45
msgid ""
"**An existing implementation**, only when there is no other source of truth. "
"This happens when the kernel's UAPI headers are wrong or unusable for user "
"space, or when an interface has no real standard and one implementation has "
"become the de facto definition, as glibc has for `termios`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:51
msgid ""
"\"A program built against another libc's headers expects this layout\" is "
"not a reason to choose that layout. Programs that rely on details a standard "
"does not promise should be fixed, unless no standard exists at all."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:55
msgid "Opaque types"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:57
msgid ""
"Where a standard allows a type to be opaque, keep it opaque. As an example, "
"LLVM-libc's `pthread_t` is a struct, which POSIX explicitly permits. Keeping "
"it a struct means its contents can change later without breaking correct "
"programs. Correct code that needs to compare thread IDs uses `pthread_equal`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:62
msgid ""
"`FILE` and `DIR` are forward declarations in the public headers. Nothing "
"outside the library can depend on their contents."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:65
msgid "Widths of common types"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:67
msgid ""
"In general it's best to have a single definition of a given type. A type "
"that differs between platforms requires extra care wherever it is handled. "
"The `long` type is an example: it is 32 or 64 bits wide depending on the "
"target. `long` is defined by the platform and the compiler, so LLVM-libc has "
"to handle both widths and cannot standardize it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:73
msgid ""
"For types that are provided by LLVM-libc, it's generally preferred to use "
"the larger type. Smaller values will fit in larger types without issue, but "
"the reverse is not true."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:77
msgid ""
"`time_t` is 64 bits on every target, because 32-bit time ends in 2038. The "
"types that travel with it, such as `off_t`, `dev_t`, and `rlim_t`, are also "
"64 bits everywhere. This simplifies the build, and it means the `*64` "
"variants of file functions are not needed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:82
msgid ""
"A few related types (`ino_t`, `nlink_t`, `blkcnt_t`) are still pointer "
"width. That is unfinished work and not a design decision."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:85
msgid "Exported symbols"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:87
msgid ""
"Every public function is defined in the internal `LIBC_NAMESPACE` namespace "
"and exported under its standard C name by the `LLVM_LIBC_FUNCTION` macro "
"(see {ref}`implementation_standard`). The default namespace name includes "
"the LLVM version, and {ref}`Hand-in-Hand <hand_in_hand>` consumers compile "
"the shared code under a different namespace, so several copies of LLVM-libc "
"code can be linked into one program. Nothing may depend on the mangled names "
"of internal symbols."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:95
msgid "Aliases for another libc's symbols"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:97
msgid "LLVM-libc does not export another libc's private symbols in general."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:99
msgid ""
"The exception is the `LIBC_COPT_PROVIDE_GNU_ALIASES` option, which is "
"enabled for Linux. It provides a short list of glibc symbol names, such as "
"`__errno_location`, because some users must link precompiled libraries that "
"were built against glibc. The list is kept short deliberately. It is a "
"compromise for those users and not a step toward binary compatibility with "
"glibc. Additions need agreement from the maintainers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:106
msgid ""
"Symbols that a compiler or a C++ standard library requires of any libc, such "
"as `__stack_chk_fail` or `__cxa_thread_atexit_impl`, are ordinary "
"entrypoints and are not covered by this restriction."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:110
msgid "Customization points"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:112
msgid ""
"Some macros exist so that downstream users can control how LLVM-libc code is "
"compiled into their own library. Downstream users depend on `LIBC_INLINE` "
"and `LIBC_INLINE_VAR` being used on every inline function and variable, and "
"`LLVM_LIBC_FUNCTION_ATTR` lets a packager attach attributes to every public "
"function. Use these macros as documented in {ref}`code_style`, and do not "
"remove or bypass them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:119
msgid "Kernel headers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:121
msgid "A full build for Linux requires the kernel's UAPI headers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:123
msgid ""
"A public header may include a UAPI header when that header is self-contained "
"and does not collide with LLVM-libc's own definitions. When a UAPI header "
"redefines a type LLVM-libc provides, or defines macros with different "
"spellings, define the needed constants and types in `llvm-libc-macros/` or "
"`llvm-libc-types/` instead. For example, UAPI headers that include `<linux/"
"fcntl.h>` redefine `struct flock` and so cannot be included."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:130
msgid "Dynamic loading"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:132
msgid ""
"LLVM-libc is linked statically today. Dynamic loading is under development, "
"mainly so that `dlopen` can work. It is not intended to become the primary "
"way to use the library, and new code should be designed for the static case "
"first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:136
msgid "Other operating systems"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:138
msgid ""
"The points above describe Linux and baremetal targets, where LLVM-libc is "
"the whole C library. A full build for macOS or Windows has not been designed "
"yet. The likely direction is for LLVM-libc to define its own API there too, "
"shaped appropriately for the platform, and not to reproduce POSIX on every "
"system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:145
msgid "Writing public headers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:147
msgid ""
"Public headers are read by every program and by compilers in every language "
"mode, so they are held to stricter rules than implementation code:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:150
msgid ""
"Declarations are not hidden behind feature-test macros such as "
"`_GNU_SOURCE`. See {ref}`feature_test_macros`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:152
msgid ""
"A header must be valid in every C and C++ standard mode. Do not use language "
"features that some of those modes lack, such as attribute syntax "
"(`[[...]]`), which C gained only in C23."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:155
msgid ""
"Names that the standard does not define must be reserved identifiers. This "
"includes structure members, macro parameters, and helper macros. A member "
"named `mutex` or a macro named `NUM_LOCALE_CATEGORIES` collides with user "
"code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:159
msgid ""
"Type headers in `llvm-libc-types/` define one type each and include only the "
"other type headers they need. Use the compiler's built-in type macros (for "
"example `__INT64_TYPE__`) instead of including `<stdint.h>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:163
msgid "Proposing an ABI change"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../abi_policy.md:165
msgid ""
"To propose a non-trivial change to the layout of an existing public type or "
"the value of an existing public macro, or to export a symbol under a name "
"other than its standard one, contributors should start by reading {ref}"
"`choosing_type_layout`, then reach out to the maintainers with the "
"justification and/or write an RFC with the new design. A demo PR may be "
"helpful for explaining the design, but should not be expected to land "
"without significant changes."
msgstr ""
